WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.529
EDR is powerful, but it is not magic.

00:00:03.529 --> 00:00:09.530
It can be misconfigured, ignored, bypassed, overwhelmed by alerts, or limited by poor coverage.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:13.816
If endpoints are unmanaged, offline, excluded, or not sending telemetry,

00:00:13.816 --> 00:00:15.959
defenders may have blind spots.

00:00:15.959 --> 00:00:21.102
EDR works best when it is deployed broadly, tuned carefully, monitored consistently,

00:00:21.102 --> 00:00:23.674
and connected to incident response processes.

00:00:23.674 --> 00:00:27.532
Good practices include maintaining endpoint coverage, reviewing high-value alerts,

00:00:27.532 --> 00:00:30.103
tuning noisy detections, documenting response procedures,

00:00:30.103 --> 00:00:33.104
integrating with SIEM or SOAR where appropriate,

00:00:33.104 --> 00:00:36.104
and training analysts to investigate endpoint behavior.

00:00:36.104 --> 00:00:39.533
EDR is one layer in a defense-in-depth strategy.

00:00:39.533 --> 00:00:44.676
It does not replace patching, identity security, network monitoring, backups, user awareness,

00:00:44.676 --> 00:00:47.248
secure configuration, or incident response planning.

00:00:47.248 --> 00:00:51.534
Its value comes from reliable visibility, useful detections, practiced response,

00:00:51.534 --> 00:00:52.820
and human judgment.
